The resulting dataset (the top of which is shown above), now has many more rows than the initial dataset, since it basically is creating duplicate versions of the dataset, one for each unique value in the .split column.
For this experiment, let’s define five models:

a random walk: this is what it sounds like. Each forecast takes a random walk from the last observed value. This is equivalent to an ARIMA(0,1,0) model.
the ARIMA(2,0,0), also sometimes just shortened into AR(2), model that we fit earlier.
a seasonal baseline model that just has fourier terms and no ARIMA terms.
a model that has both the AR(2) and fourier terms.
a copycat model: a probabilistic method of analogues. Rather than extrapolating a parametric time-series model, it matches the current season’s recent growth pattern against a library of past influenza seasons and reuses the subsequent growth of the closest analogues, re-anchored to the most recent observed value.







Tip



The COPYCAT() model and its analogue library copycat_db come with the course package. The library holds every US influenza season before 2015/16 (National, the HHS regions, and the states), drawn from ILINet. It stores each season as a log weekly growth rate by week-of-season, which is scale-free — so seasons from locations of very different sizes are directly comparable, and the model can forecast the national wILI series directly (no transformation needed). It is a reimplementation of the copycat model by Spencer Fox / epiENGAGE. The copycat model is similar in spirit to other method of analogue implementations for infectious disease forecasting.(Viboud et al. 2003; Ray et al. 2017)


The following code generates the forecasts for each cross-validation split and for all horizons.

cv_forecasts &lt;- flu_data_tscv |&gt;
  model(
    rw = RW(my_fourth_root(wili)),
    ar2 = ARIMA(my_fourth_root(wili) ~ pdq(2,0,0)),
    fourier = ARIMA(my_fourth_root(wili) ~ pdq(0,0,0) + fourier(period = "year", K=3)),
    fourier_ar2 = ARIMA(my_fourth_root(wili) ~ pdq(2,0,0) + fourier(period = "year", K=3)),
    copycat = COPYCAT(wili)
  ) |&gt;
  forecast(h = 8) |&gt; 
  ## the following 3 lines of code ensure that there is a horizon variable in the forecast data
  group_by(.split, .model) |&gt; 
  mutate(h = row_number()) |&gt; 
  ungroup() |&gt; 
  ## this ensures that the output is a table object
  as_fable(response = "wili", distribution = wili)

A common mistake in forecasting is to make forecasts and then just skip to looking at the metrics! Let’s pause here to look at a few of the forecasts.

cv_forecasts |&gt;
  filter(.split == 1) |&gt; 
  tsibble::update_tsibble(key=c(.model, region)) |&gt;  
  as_fable(response = "wili", distribution = wili) |&gt; 
  autoplot(flu_data |&gt; filter(epiweek &gt;= as.Date("2016-09-01"))) +
  facet_wrap(~.model) +
  labs(title = "WILI, US level",
       y="% of visits")









